  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 358 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nRidging will persist through Thursday across the Intermountain \nWest. This will place the central plains in west northwesterly \nflow aloft, leading to mild and dry conditions across the area. \nRidging will begin to transition east Friday into Saturday as a \nbroad trough of low pressure deepens over the Pacific Northwest.\nDaily high temperatures will generally be in the 70s with lower\n80s possible over southwestern Nebraska Tuesday and Saturday. \nPrecipitation chances will be almost zero in the long term \nperiods. The latest NBM ensemble forecast has a near zero chance\nof measurable precipitation from Monday night through Saturday.\nLooking beyond Saturday, there are hints at a pattern shift \nlate Sunday into Monday the following week. A broad trough of \nlow pressure over the Pacific Northwest, will transition east \nacross southern Canada and the northern tier of states. The \nlatest EC and GFS forces a strong cold front through the area \neither Sunday or Sunday night based on model choice. High \ntemperatures for Sunday and beyond, as depicted in the NBM \nensemble, have a high spread. On Monday (October 12), the 25th \nto 75th %ile spread in highs for North Platte ranges from 51 to \n73 degrees. Additionally, the deterministic GFS and EC solns \ntonight develop some light precipitation across northern \nNebraska late Sunday into Monday.",
